@NODE Introduction "Introduction"
@TOC Main
@PREV Main
@NEXT Requirements
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Introduction"

   @{b}What is CurrentPod?@{ub}

   CurrentPod is a podcast client (podcatcher) for AmigaOS 3.0 and
   later with a MUI interface. It subscribes to podcast feeds (RSS 2.0
   with Apple's itunes: extension, or Atom), downloads episodes over
   HTTP and HTTPS and plays them right on the Amiga with mpega.library,
   straight out through Paula in 14 bits.

   The program is an ordinary MUI program with menus, tooltypes and a
   couple of windows, written in C in 2026 but meant to feel at home on
   a Workbench 3.1 from 1997. It uses the libraries that have arrived
   since then where they are needed: AmiSSL for encrypted connections
   and mpega.library (1998) for the decoding.

   @{b}Compatible with Apple Podcasts@{ub}

   "Compatible with Apple Podcasts" means three things:

      1. You can @{"search" link Catalog} the Apple Podcasts catalog right in the
         program, in the country of your choice, and subscribe to a hit
         with one click. The catalog is queried through Apple's open
         search interface (the iTunes Search API), which needs no
         account.

      2. You can @{"paste a link" link Subscribe} from Apple Podcasts
         (podcasts.apple.com/...). CurrentPod looks up the podcast ID
         and finds the real RSS feed by itself.

      3. Feeds are parsed with the same rules Apple uses: itunes: tags
         for artwork, duration, season, episode number, episode type
         (full/trailer/bonus) and the explicit flag.

   It does @{i}not@{ui} mean that CurrentPod signs in with an Apple ID, syncs
   your listening position with Apple's servers or plays paid podcasts.

   @{b}What is it not?@{ub}

   CurrentPod is not a general MP3 player. It plays the episodes it
   knows about, and remembers where you were. For loose MP3 files there
   is HippoPlayer with the mpega plugin, or AmigaAMP.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Requirements "Requirements"
@TOC Main
@PREV Introduction
@NEXT Installation
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Requirements"

   @{b}Minimum@{ub}

      - AmigaOS 3.0 or later (Kickstart 39+)
      - 68020 or better, 2 MB fast RAM, 1 MB free chip memory
        (the audio buffers take 192 KB of chip)
      - MUI 3.8 or later (on this machine MUI 3.9)
      - A TCP/IP stack with bsdsocket.library (in the emulator: the
        one built into FS-UAE; on real hardware AmiTCP, Miami, Genesis
        or Roadshow)
      - AmiSSL 4.x or 5.x (amisslmaster.library) for HTTPS, which Apple
        and most podcast servers require
      - mpega.library 2.0 or later in LIBS: (for playback)

   @{b}Recommended@{ub}

      - A 68030 at 50 MHz with FPU, preferably more. See @{"Performance" link Performance}.
      - A hard disk with room: episodes are 20-80 MB each.

   @{b}What can the machine handle?@{ub}

   MP3 decoding (layer III) is the heavy part. CurrentPod has four
   quality levels and automatically picks the highest one the decoder
   can keep up with:

      Low      mono, 11-12 kHz, mpega quality 0
      Medium   mono, 22-24 kHz, quality 0
      High     stereo, 22-24 kHz, quality 1
      Full     stereo, 44-48 kHz, quality 2

   For speech the difference between Medium and Full is small. What
   the various machines can manage is listed under @{"Performance" link Performance}.

   Fetching and parsing feeds works fine on any 020; it is only the
   playback that wants horsepower.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Installation "Installation"
@TOC Main
@PREV Requirements
@NEXT QuickStart
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Installation"

   On this machine CurrentPod is already installed in
   @{b}Work:Internet/CurrentPod@{ub}, and mpega.library is in LIBS:. There is
   no installation script: the program drawer is self-contained.

   @{b}By hand on another Amiga@{ub}

   Copy the CurrentPod drawer wherever you want it. Put mpega.library
   (Aminet util/libs/mpega_library.lha; take the variant for your
   processor, 020FPU for an 030 with FPU, 060FPU for an 060) in LIBS:
   under the name mpega.library. Install AmiSSL from Aminet with its
   own script.

   The program finds everything through PROGDIR:, so no assign is
   needed. On the first start PROGDIR:Feeds (the database) and
   PROGDIR:Episodes (downloaded files) are created. If you want the
   episodes on another partition, set the DOWNLOADDIR tooltype.

   @{b}Uninstalling@{ub}

   Delete the drawer. CurrentPod writes nothing anywhere else, except
   ENV:MUI/CURRENTPOD.cfg if you have saved MUI settings for the
   program.

@ENDNODE

@NODE QuickStart "Quick start"
@TOC Main
@PREV Installation
@NEXT MainWindow
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Quick start"

   This is how you get your first podcast going. The network should be
   up; IBrowse should be able to show a page, for example.

      1. Double-click the CurrentPod icon in Work:Internet/CurrentPod.

      2. Choose @{b}Podcast -> Search Apple Podcasts...@{ub} (or press Amiga-F).
         Type a search term, for example "retro hour", pick a country
         in the cycle gadget and press Return. After a while up to 50
         hits are shown with name, publisher and number of episodes.
         The search goes over HTTPS and takes 20-40 seconds on an 030.

      3. Double-click a hit (or select it and click @{b}Subscribe@{ub}). The
         podcast lands in the left list and the feed is fetched in the
         background. The Retro Hour's feed is 1.7 MB and takes a couple
         of minutes over HTTPS on an 030; the status line shows how it
         is going.

      4. Select an episode in the right list and click @{b}Download@{ub} below
         the list (or press Amiga-D). The progress shows in the status
         column and the status line. It takes its time: count on 30-35
         kB/s over HTTPS, a good hour for a two-hour episode. Do
         something else meanwhile; the download does not get in the
         way.

      5. When the episode is downloaded the status column says
         "downloaded" and the @{b}Play@{ub} and @{b}Convert@{ub} buttons light up. Click
         Play (or double-click the row). Space pauses, cursor right
         skips 30 seconds forward, cursor left 15 back.

      6. If the sound stutters, the machine cannot decode MP3 in real
         time. Click @{b}Convert@{ub}: the episode is turned into an 8SVX file in
         the background, and Play then uses that instead. See
         @{"Converting" link Convert}.

   The next time you start the program it remembers where you were in
   the episode and starts there (minus three seconds) if you play it
   again.

   @{b}Tip@{ub}: if you have the address of a feed you can skip the search and
   use @{b}Podcast -> Subscribe to address...@{ub} (Amiga-S).

@ENDNODE

@NODE MainWindow "The main window"
@TOC Main
@PREV QuickStart
@NEXT Subscribe
@TITLE "CurrentPod - The main window"

   The main window has three parts. The split between the left and
   right lists can be dragged with MUI's balance bar, and the window's
   size and position are saved with the MUI settings (snapshot in the
   MUI menu).

   +--------------------+------------------------------------------------+
   | Podcast        New | Status     Date         Episode         Length |
   |                    |                                                |
   | The Retro Hou   50 | new        16 Oct 2026  553. Gremlin G..  2:04 |
   | Kodsnack         3 | downloaded 10 Oct 2026  552. GamesMast..  1:48 |
   |                    | 8SVX        2 Oct 2026  551. Streets o..  1:18 |
   |                    |                                                |
   |                    +------------------------------------------------+
   |                    |   [ Download ]     [ Play ]      [ Convert ]   |
   +--------------------+------------------------------------------------+
   | [|<] [<<] [ > ] [||] [>>] [>|] 553. Gremlin...  12:31 / 2:04:16     |
   | [===========|---------------------------------]  Vol [=======|-]    |
   |         128 kbit/s -> 24000 Hz mono, 14 bit, quality medium         |
   +---------------------------------------------------------------------+
   | Fetching 14.2 MB / 31.8 MB: 552. GamesMaster...                     |
   +---------------------------------------------------------------------+

   @{b}The podcast list (left)@{ub}

   One row per subscription, with the number of unheard episodes at
   the right edge. Bold: the feed has new episodes since you last
   looked. Italic: the feed is being updated right now. A "!" in the
   right column means the latest update failed; the error shows in the
   status line and in @{b}Podcast -> Information...@{ub}.

   @{b}The episode list (right)@{ub}

   The episodes of the selected podcast, newest first (oldest first
   for podcasts that have marked themselves as "serial"). The columns
   are status, date, title and length. See @{"The episode list" link Episodes} for
   the status column.

   @{b}The button row below the episode list@{ub}

   The three buttons follow the state of the selected episode:

      Download    Lit until the episode is downloaded. Puts it in the
                  download queue.
      Play        Lit when the episode is downloaded. Plays the 8SVX
                  file if there is one, otherwise the MP3 file directly.
      Convert     Lit when the episode is downloaded and not converted.
                  Makes an 8SVX file in the background; goes dark when
                  it is done or while another conversion is running.

   Double-clicking a row means Play if the episode is downloaded,
   otherwise Download. An episode that is not downloaded cannot be
   played: the Play button in the player panel then says so in the
   status line.

   @{b}The player panel@{ub}

      [|<]  Previous downloaded episode in the list (or start over, if
            more than 10 seconds have been played)
      [<<]  15 seconds back
      [ > ] Play the selected episode (must be downloaded)
      [||]  Pause / resume
      [>>]  30 seconds forward
      [>|]  Next downloaded episode

   The gauge shows the position. The volume slider works digitally in
   the decoder, so 14-bit mode keeps its full resolution. The line
   below shows the stream's bitrate, what the decoder delivers, bit
   depth and the chosen quality level. If it says CAN'T KEEP UP the
   decoder is more than 1.5 seconds behind; see @{"Performance" link Performance}.

   @{b}The status line@{ub}

   Shows the download in progress with the amount transferred, or the
   latest event.

   @{b}Menus@{ub}

   @{b}Project@{ub}
      About CurrentPod...      A-?
      Save settings                 Writes PROGDIR:CurrentPod.prefs
      Quit                     A-Q  (also saves the settings)

   @{b}Podcast@{ub}
      Search Apple Podcasts... A-F
      Subscribe to address...  A-S  RSS address or Apple link
      Update                   A-U  The selected podcast
      Update all               A-A
      Information...           A-I  Description, addresses, last error
      Remove subscription           Downloaded files stay on disk

   @{b}Episode@{ub}
      Play                     A-P
      Pause / resume
      Stop
      Download                 A-D
      Download all new              All unheard episodes in the podcast
      Cancel download
      Convert to 8SVX          A-K  See Converting
      Cancel conversion
      Delete MP3, keep 8SVX         Saves disk after converting
      Mark as heard/unheard    A-M
      Delete downloaded file        Both MP3 and 8SVX; the episode
                                    stays in the list
      Information...           A-E  The whole description
      Open web page                 In IBrowse (the BROWSER tooltype)

   @{b}Settings@{ub}
      Quality                       Automatic / Low / Medium / High / Full
      Conversion                    12 kHz, smaller file / 24 kHz
      14-bit audio                  Off: 8 bits, two Paula channels
      Play next automatically

@ENDNODE

@NODE Subscribe "Subscribing"
@TOC Main
@PREV MainWindow
@NEXT Catalog
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Subscribing"

   There are two ways to add a podcast.

   @{b}1. Search the Apple Podcasts catalog@{ub}

   The easiest. See @{"Searching the catalog" link Catalog}.

   @{b}2. Paste a link or an RSS address@{ub}

   Choose @{b}Podcast -> Subscribe to address...@{ub} or press Amiga-S. The
   field accepts any of the following:

      https://podcasts.apple.com/se/podcast/kodsnack/id561631498
      https://itunes.apple.com/se/podcast/id561631498
      https://feeds.example.se/kodsnack.xml
      feed://feeds.example.se/kodsnack.xml
      podcast://feeds.example.se/kodsnack.xml
      feeds.example.se/kodsnack.xml   (https:// is added)

   Apple links contain a podcast ID (the digits after "id"). CurrentPod
   looks the ID up at Apple and gets back the address of the real RSS
   feed, which is then fetched.

   The address can be pasted with Amiga-V if it was copied in IBrowse.

   @{b}What happens next@{ub}

   The podcast appears in the list as "(fetching...)" and the feed is
   fetched in the background. When it is done the name is replaced by
   the feed's title and the episodes are filled in, at most as many as
   MAXEPISODES says (default 50). The podcast's name, publisher,
   description, web page, artwork address and language are saved in
   PROGDIR:Feeds.

   Feeds larger than 3 MB (LINUX Unplugged with 689 episodes is 9 MB)
   are read only up to 3 MB, which is enough for the 200 newest
   episodes. The status line then says "large feed, newest 50".

   If the fetch fails the podcast stays with a "!" and can be updated
   again with Amiga-U once the problem is fixed, or removed.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Catalog "Searching the catalog"
@TOC Main
@PREV Subscribe
@NEXT Update
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Searching Apple Podcasts"

   @{b}Podcast -> Search Apple Podcasts...@{ub} (Amiga-F) opens the search
   window.

   +-----------------------------------------------------------------+
   | Search: [retro hour                       ]  Country: [Sweden ] |
   +-----------------------------------------------------------------+
   | Name                           Publisher               Episodes |
   | The Retro Hour (Retro Gaming.. The Retro Hour              500  |
   | Retro VGM Revival Hour         Retro VGM Revival Hour       95  |
   | ...                                                             |
   +-----------------------------------------------------------------+
   | 3 hits                                                          |
   | [Subscribe]                                           [Close]   |
   +-----------------------------------------------------------------+

   @{b}The search field@{ub}

   The search is sent to Apple's search interface when you press
   Return. Apple searches roughly like the Apple Podcasts app does: in
   title, publisher and description. Swedish characters work
   (CurrentPod translates from ISO-8859-1 to UTF-8 and URL-encodes).
   At most 50 hits are shown.

   @{b}Country@{ub}

   The catalog is split per country, and a podcast that only exists in
   the British catalog is not found if you search the Swedish one.
   Change country in the cycle gadget; the choice is saved in the
   settings.

   @{b}The hit list@{ub}

   Podcasts Apple has marked as "explicit" are shown with [E]. Double-
   clicking a row subscribes, as does the @{b}Subscribe@{ub} button. The
   window can stay open while you subscribe to several.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Update "Updating feeds"
@TOC Main
@PREV Catalog
@NEXT Episodes
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Updating feeds"

   A feed is updated by fetching the RSS file again and comparing it
   with the database. New episodes are added at the top and marked as
   new; episodes already present keep their status and position.

   Amiga-U updates the selected podcast, Amiga-A all of them. The
   update runs in the background, one podcast at a time, and you can
   keep listening.

   @{b}Frugality@{ub}

   CurrentPod sends If-Modified-Since and If-None-Match (ETag) if the
   server supplied them. An unchanged feed then costs only a few
   hundred bytes and the reply "no changes". At most the first 300
   episodes of a feed are parsed.

   @{b}Redirects@{ub}

   Up to six redirects (301/302/303/307/308) are followed, also
   between http and https.

   @{b}What is parsed@{ub}

   RSS 2.0 and Atom 1.0. From each episode these are taken:

      title, pubDate, enclosure (address, size, type), guid, link,
      description or content:encoded (HTML is stripped; p and br
      become line breaks), itunes:title, itunes:duration,
      itunes:episode, itunes:season, itunes:episodeType,
      itunes:explicit, itunes:image, itunes:summary, media:content
      (if there is no enclosure)

   Feeds in UTF-8 are translated to ISO-8859-1; characters that do not
   exist there (emoji and the like) become question marks, and
   typographic quotes and dashes become plain ones.

   An episode without an audio file is shown in italics and cannot be
   downloaded.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Episodes "The episode list"
@TOC Main
@PREV Update
@NEXT Download
@TITLE "CurrentPod - The episode list"

   @{b}The status column@{ub}

   The first column shows the episode's state in plain words:

      new             New since the last update, not downloaded
      (empty)         Unheard, not downloaded
      queued          Waiting in the download queue
      fetching 34%    Being downloaded right now
      downloaded      Downloaded (MP3). An episode you have started
                      also shows as downloaded; the position shows
                      when you play it
      conv. 37%       Being converted to 8SVX right now
      8SVX            Downloaded and converted: plays without decoding
      heard           Heard (all the way, or marked as heard)
      heard, deleted  Heard and the file deleted
      playing/paused  Playing right now (bold)
      no file         The feed has no audio file for the episode
      error           The download failed (the error is in the status
                      line)

   If the column is too narrow for the percentage, drag the window
   wider or read the status line, which shows the same thing with
   amount and time.

   @{b}Date@{ub}

   pubDate from the feed. Feeds without dates get today's date.

   @{b}Title@{ub}

   itunes:title if present, otherwise title. The episode number is
   shown as "553." before the title, season and episode as "S2E14".
   Trailers get "[Trailer]" and bonus episodes "[Bonus]".

   @{b}Length@{ub}

   itunes:duration. If it is missing the file size is shown instead.

   @{b}Episode information@{ub}

   Amiga-E or @{b}Episode -> Information...@{ub} opens a window with the whole
   description, the address of the audio file and where it is on disk.

   @{b}Marking as heard@{ub}

   An episode counts as heard when it has been played to the end.
   Amiga-M toggles it by hand.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Download "Downloading episodes"
@TOC Main
@PREV Episodes
@NEXT Playback
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Downloading episodes"

   @{b}Download first, play later@{ub}

   An episode must be downloaded completely before it can be played.
   CurrentPod does not stream: at 30 kB/s over HTTPS it would only be
   enough for the lowest quality anyway, and a hiccup on the network
   would become a hiccup in the sound. That is why the Play button
   stays dark until the file is on disk.

   @{b}The download queue@{ub}

   The @{b}Download@{ub} button (or Amiga-D) puts the selected episode in the
   queue. The queue runs one file at a time, and it shares the network
   process with feed updates and searches, so a search has to wait
   until the current download is done (or cancelled with @{b}Episode ->
   Cancel download@{ub}).

   The download runs in its own process with a lower priority (-1)
   than the interface. The player has priority +1 as long as it keeps
   up, so the sound comes before the download.

   @{b}Speed@{ub}

   HTTPS files are fetched at 30-35 kB/s on an 030 at 50 MHz; AmiSSL's
   decryption is the bottleneck, not the line or the disk (plain HTTP
   goes at over 200 kB/s). CurrentPod therefore negotiates ChaCha20,
   which is almost three times faster than AES on a 68030, and falls
   back to AES if the server cannot. A 120 MB episode still takes an
   hour. The status column shows the percentage and the status line
   the amount.

   @{b}Resuming@{ub}

   If the connection breaks midway (CloudFront does that after 10-20
   minutes) CurrentPod carries on by itself from where it was with a
   Range request, up to 60 times as long as it makes progress. Three
   attempts in a row without a single new byte give up with the error
   in the status line; Download again then continues from the same
   spot. Partially downloaded files have the extension .part instead
   of .mp3.

   @{b}Where do the files go?@{ub}

   In the drawer DOWNLOADDIR points to (default PROGDIR:Episodes), one
   subdrawer per podcast:

      Work:Internet/CurrentPod/Episodes/The Retro Hour (Retro/
         2026-10-16 553. Gremli.mp3

   The file name is built from date and title and shortened to fit in
   FFS's 30 characters. The characters / : # ? are replaced by
   underscores.

   @{b}Deleting@{ub}

   @{b}Episode -> Delete downloaded file@{ub} (Del) deletes the MP3 file and any
   8SVX file but keeps the episode in the list. The listening position
   is reset. @{b}Delete MP3, keep 8SVX@{ub} removes only the MP3, for those who
   have converted and want their disk back.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Playback "Playback"
@TOC Main
@PREV Download
@NEXT Convert
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Playback"

   @{b}How it works@{ub}

   Playback runs in its own process, which decodes the MP3 stream with
   mpega.library, buffer by buffer, and feeds the sound to Paula
   through audio.device with three queued buffers of 16384 samples per
   channel. The main program talks to the player process with
   messages, so the interface does not lock up when the decoder has
   its hands full.

   ID3v2 tags at the start of the file (often hundreds of kilobytes of
   cover art) are skipped before the decoder gets to see the stream.

   @{b}14 bits@{ub}

   Paula has 8 bits per channel, but with two channels per side, one
   at volume 64 and one at volume 1, you get 14 bits. CurrentPod does
   this by default. The volume is then controlled digitally in the
   decoder (MPEGA_scale) so that Paula always runs at full volume.
   With "14-bit audio" switched off two channels are used in 8 bits,
   which sounds worse but leaves two channels free.

   @{b}Quality levels@{ub}

   Under Settings -> Quality there are five choices:

      Automatic   Starts at the level for your processor, measures the
                  first buffer and lowers until the decoder keeps up.
                  If it cannot keep up even on Low, the player's
                  priority is lowered so that the interface and the
                  network keep working.
      Low         Mono, 11-12 kHz, mpega quality 0.
      Medium      Mono, 22-24 kHz, quality 0.
      High        Stereo, 22-24 kHz, quality 1.
      Full        Stereo, 44-48 kHz, quality 2.

   The level applies from the next episode started. The output rate is
   the stream's rate divided by 1, 2 or 4, so a 48 kHz podcast gives
   24 kHz on Medium and a 44.1 kHz podcast gives 22 kHz.

   @{b}Position@{ub}

   The position is saved in the database every 15 seconds and when you
   switch episode or quit. If you start a partly heard episode it
   continues three seconds before where you stopped. Seeking in the
   file uses mpega.library's time-based seek, which is approximate in
   files with variable bitrate.

   @{b}Skipping@{ub}

   Cursor right skips 30 seconds forward, cursor left 15 seconds back.
   The times can be changed in the prefs file (SkipForward, SkipBack).

   @{b}Playing on to the next@{ub}

   When an episode ends it is marked as heard, and the next downloaded
   episode in the list is played if "Play next automatically" is on.

   @{b}Converted episodes@{ub}

   If there is an 8SVX file for the episode, Play plays it instead of
   the MP3: same process and buttons, but no decoding at all, so it
   works on any Amiga. The quality level does not apply then, and the
   line below the gauge says "8SVX 12000 Hz mono, 8 bit, no decoding".
   The volume is controlled in Paula. See @{"Converting" link Convert}.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Convert "Converting to 8SVX"
@TOC Main
@PREV Playback
@NEXT Performance
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Converting to 8SVX"

   @{b}Why@{ub}

   A 68030 can only just decode MP3 in real time in mono, and an
   unaccelerated A1200 cannot do it at all. But every Amiga can play
   8-bit samples from disk without breaking a sweat. Converting does
   the decoding once, in the background, and saves the result as an
   ordinary IFF 8SVX file next to the MP3. After that, playback is
   just disk to Paula.

   @{b}How@{ub}

   Select a downloaded episode and click @{b}Convert@{ub} (or Amiga-K). The
   status column shows "conv. 37%" and the status line the percentage
   and the converted playing time. You can keep using the program
   meanwhile, and even play the MP3 directly. The conversion runs in
   its own process with priority -2, lowest of all, so it only takes
   the time left over from interface, network and player.

   When it is done the status column says "8SVX", Convert goes dark,
   and Play uses the 8SVX file. @{b}Episode -> Cancel conversion@{ub} cancels;
   the half-finished .tmp file is deleted.

   @{b}How long@{ub}

   The decoder works at the same pace as during playback: on an 030 at
   50 MHz, 12 kHz takes about as long as the episode itself, 24 kHz
   about one and a half times as long. A 1260 does it in a third of
   that. Convert in the evening, listen in the morning.

   @{b}Format and size@{ub}

   Under @{b}Settings -> Conversion@{ub} you choose the output:

      12 kHz, smaller file   12 kB per second: 85 MB for two hours.
                             Telephone quality plus; good enough for
                             speech.
      24 kHz, better sound   24 kB per second: 170 MB for two hours.
                             Like a good Amiga sample.

   The rate is the stream's divided by 4 or 2 respectively, so a
   44.1 kHz podcast gives 11 or 22 kHz. Always mono, 8 bits,
   uncompressed. The file has the same name as the MP3 with the
   extension .8svx and can be played by any sample player:
   HippoPlayer, DeliTracker, MultiView. The Pcm field in the feed file
   points to it.

   @{b}Disk@{ub}

   The MP3 stays after the conversion. @{b}Episode -> Delete MP3, keep
   8SVX@{ub} removes it once you are happy; @{b}Delete downloaded file@{ub} takes
   both. An episode whose MP3 has been deleted still shows "8SVX" and
   can be played but not converted again.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Performance "Performance"
@TOC Main
@PREV Convert
@NEXT Settings
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Performance"

   The figures below were measured in the player's debug log with The
   Retro Hour (128 kbit/s, 48 kHz, joint stereo) and the 020+FPU build
   of mpega.library. "Percent of real time" is decoding time divided
   by the buffer's playing time; below 100 it works, above it
   stutters.

   @{b}The emulator with CPU throttle (like 1997)@{ub}

      Config              Medium (24 kHz mono)  Low (12 kHz)  High (stereo)
      A1200 + 030/50        160 %                  78 %          -
      A1200 + 1260           80 %                  -            125 %

   @{b}The emulator without throttle@{ub}

      A1200 + 030            50 %                  -             -

   The conclusion: with the throttle, which mimics a real 68030 at
   50 MHz, the 1260 config plays podcasts in mono without trouble,
   and the 030 config manages the lowest level (12 kHz mono). The
   throttle is a plain slowdown of the processor (uae_cpu_throttle)
   and does not account for FPU code having been comparatively faster
   on a real 68882. A real Blizzard 1230 IV plays 22 kHz mono in
   practice, and a 1260 full stereo. If it stutters: convert to 8SVX,
   see @{"Converting" link Convert}.

   @{b}What can you do?@{ub}

      - Run the podcasts on the 1260 config (./run.sh dream).
      - Ease off the throttle in a1200-1997.fs-uae (uae_cpu_throttle,
        a smaller negative number), or remove it entirely: then even
        the 030 config handles Medium with a good margin.
      - Pick podcasts with a lower bitrate: the decoding time depends
        partly on the number of bits per frame.
      - Download episodes when nothing is playing. The network process
        takes processor time too, and it halves the decoding speed on
        the 030.
      - Convert the episodes to 8SVX (the Convert button): then even
        an unaccelerated A1200 plays them. See @{"Converting" link Convert}.

   The program itself spends almost no time outside mpega.library: the
   conversion to 14 bits and the chip memory writes are under two
   percent of the buffer time.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Settings "Settings"
@TOC Main
@PREV Performance
@NEXT Tooltypes
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Settings"

   Settings are made in the @{b}Settings@{ub} menu (quality, conversion, 14
   bits, play next automatically), in the search window (country) and
   with the volume slider. They are saved in PROGDIR:CurrentPod.prefs
   on @{b}Project -> Save settings@{ub} and when the program quits. The rest of
   the settings are set as @{"tooltypes" link Tooltypes} or directly in the prefs
   file, which is plain text:

      DownloadDir=Work:Internet/CurrentPod/Episodes
      Quality=0            0 auto, 1 low, 2 medium, 3 high, 4 full
      ConvertQuality=1     1: 12 kHz, 2: 24 kHz (8SVX)
      Bits14=1
      Country=se
      MaxEpisodes=50
      Verify=0             1: verify TLS certificates (see Troubleshooting)
      AutoUpdate=0         (not in use yet)
      SkipForward=30
      SkipBack=15
      Volume=64
      AutoNext=1
      Browser=Work:Internet/IBrowse/IBrowse "%s"

   Tooltypes in the icon take precedence over the prefs file.

   Fonts, colours, frames and window positions are set in the MUI
   settings, as for every MUI program. The window IDs are CPD1 (the
   main window), CPD2 (search), CPD3 (address) and CPD4 (information).

@ENDNODE

@NODE Tooltypes "Tooltypes"
@TOC Main
@PREV Settings
@NEXT Keys
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Tooltypes"

   The following tooltypes can be set in the icon (Workbench ->
   Information) or given as Shell arguments in the form NAME=value.

      DOWNLOADDIR=path     Drawer for episodes (default PROGDIR:Episodes)
      QUALITY=AUTO|LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH|FULL
      CONVERT=LOW|MEDIUM   8SVX at 12 or 24 kHz
      BITS=14|8
      COUNTRY=xx           Country code for the catalog (se, no, dk,
                           fi, de, gb, us, fr, nl, pl)
      MAXEPISODES=n        Episodes per podcast in the list
      VERIFY=0|1           Verify TLS certificates
      BROWSER=command      With %s for the address
      URL=address          Subscribe to the address at startup
      LANGUAGE=svenska     Swedish interface, regardless of the Locale
                           preferences
      DEBUG                Log to PROGDIR:debug.log

   The program is in English by default. When the Workbench language
   in the Locale preferences is svenska it uses the Swedish catalog,
   Catalogs/svenska/CurrentPod.catalog, by itself; LANGUAGE=svenska
   picks that catalog no matter what Locale says.

   Shell example:

      Stack 65536
      Run >NIL: Work:Internet/CurrentPod/CurrentPod QUALITY=LOW DEBUG

   The program switches to a 64 KB stack by itself if the one it got
   is smaller, so the Stack line is not necessary but does no harm.

   In the Shell there is also PLAYFILE=file, which plays an MP3 file
   directly without a podcast. It is meant for testing the player.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Keys "Keyboard shortcuts"
@TOC Main
@PREV Tooltypes
@NEXT Files
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Keyboard shortcuts"

   @{b}The main window@{ub}

      Space            Play / pause (plays the selected episode if
                       nothing is playing)
      Return           Play the selected episode (if downloaded)
      Cursor right     30 s forward
      Cursor left      15 s back
      Cursor up/down   Move in the active list
      Tab              Switch between the podcast and episode lists
      Del              Delete downloaded file
      Amiga-F          Search Apple Podcasts
      Amiga-S          Subscribe to address
      Amiga-U          Update podcast       Amiga-A: all
      Amiga-D          Download episode
      Amiga-K          Convert to 8SVX
      Amiga-M          Heard / unheard
      Amiga-P          Play
      Amiga-I          Podcast information  Amiga-E: episode
      Amiga-?          About CurrentPod
      Amiga-Q          Quit

   On the Linux keyboard in the emulator the left Windows key is the
   right Amiga key.

   MUI's own keys (Esc closes windows, Amiga-Alt-key for window size
   and so on) work as usual.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Files "Files and formats"
@TOC Main
@PREV Keys
@NEXT Troubleshooting
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Files and formats"

   The program drawer contains:

      CurrentPod                The program (68020+, compiled with gcc)
      CurrentPod.guide          This manual
      CurrentPod_svenska.guide  The Swedish manual
      CurrentPod.prefs          Settings, plain text (created on save)
      Catalogs/svenska/
         CurrentPod.catalog     The Swedish translation of the interface
      Feeds/                    The database
         feeds.idx              One line per podcast: number and name
         0001.feed ...          The podcast's metadata and episode list
      Episodes/                 Downloaded episodes, one drawer per podcast:
         *.mp3                     downloaded episodes
         *.part                    downloads in progress
         *.8svx                    converted episodes (IFF 8SVX)
         *.tmp                     conversion in progress
      debug.log                 Only with DEBUG

   @{b}The feed files@{ub}

   Each .feed file is plain text in ISO-8859-1 with one line per
   field, so it can be read and edited with an ordinary editor if
   something has gone odd. The start of a file:

      CURRENTPOD-FEED 1
      Name=The Retro Hour (Retro Gaming Podcast)
      Url=https://audioboom.com/channels/4970769.rss
      Site=http://theretrohour.com
      Author=The Retro Hour (Retro Gaming Podcast)
      Lang=en
      Image=https://audioboom.com/i/37945576.png
      Apple=1073270208
      ETag=W/"204980f4abfa7bc870478f83bd3a4e35"
      Modified=Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:05:33 GMT
      Updated=18-Oct-26 13:35:53
      AutoDownload=0
      Serial=0
      Desc=Your weekly dose of retro gaming and technology news...
      .
      [E]
      Guid=audioboom-01a14494-22a1-741d-9c5c-9c046eef2bdf
      Title=Gremlin Graphics Is Back: What Happens Next? - EP553
      Date=2026-10-16 11:58:41
      Enc=https://pscrb.fm/rss/p/dts.podtrac.com/redirect.mp3/...
      Size=0
      Type=audio/mpeg
      Link=https://audioboom.com/posts/8965651
      Image=https://audioboom.com/i/42371889.png
      Dur=7456
      Season=0
      Episode=553
      EType=0
      Explicit=0
      State=2
      Pos=0
      New=1
      File=Work:Internet/CurrentPod/Episodes/The Retro Hour (Retro/...
      Pcm=Work:Internet/CurrentPod/Episodes/The Retro Hour (Retro/...
      Desc=Gremlin Graphics is making a comeback...\n\nContents:...
      .

   State: 0 unheard, 1 queued, 2 downloaded, 3 heard, 4 error, 5
   partly heard, 6 being converted (becomes 2 at the next start). Pos
   is in seconds. File is the MP3 file and Pcm the 8SVX file; if the
   file is missing on disk at startup the field is removed. Line
   breaks in Desc are written as "\n".

   The program writes the database on every change, first to a
   temporary file which is then renamed, so a power cut does not wreck
   the feed. Back up the Feeds drawer if you want to keep your
   listening history.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@TOC Main
@PREV Files
@NEXT KnownProblems
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Troubleshooting"

   Start with the DEBUG tooltype and the program writes a detailed log
   to PROGDIR:debug.log: every step of a download, the decoder's
   settings and how long each buffer took.

   @{b}"Could not open bsdsocket.library: is the TCP/IP stack running?"@{ub}

   In the emulator: bsdsocket_library = 1 is missing from the
   configuration. On real hardware: start Miami/AmiTCP/Roadshow.

   @{b}"AmiSSL is missing (amisslmaster.library)"@{ub}

   LIBS:amisslmaster.library is missing or too old, or LIBS:AmiSSL/
   lacks amissl_v5xx.library for your processor.

   @{b}"DNS lookup failed"@{ub}

   The stack cannot resolve names. If IBrowse can show the page the
   problem is temporary.

   @{b}"The certificate could not be verified"@{ub}

   Only with VERIFY=1. AmiSSL's root certificates are too old for the
   server, or the clock is wrong. The default is not to verify.

   @{b}"The TLS handshake failed"@{ub}

   The server requires something AmiSSL cannot do. CurrentPod limits
   itself to TLS 1.2, since TLS 1.3 hangs after the handshake in
   AmiSSL under FS-UAE's bsdsocket emulation. Nearly all servers still
   allow 1.2.

   @{b}"The server replied 403"@{ub}

   Apple sometimes rejects too many searches in a short time. Wait a
   while.

   @{b}"The feed could not be parsed (not RSS?)"@{ub}

   Usually a feed that is not RSS at all (an HTML page, an error
   page). Open the address in IBrowse and see what comes back.

   @{b}"Timeout: the server does not respond"@{ub}

   No bytes in 60 seconds. Try again.

   @{b}"The audio channels are busy (another program playing?)"@{ub}

   Something else is holding Paula: HippoPlayer, ProTracker,
   DeliTracker. Stop it and play again. CurrentPod releases the
   channels when it stops.

   @{b}"mpega.library is missing in LIBS:"@{ub}

   Put the right variant from Aminet util/libs/mpega_library.lha in
   LIBS: under the name mpega.library. Note that an Amiga library must
   be called what it says inside the file, so the variants cannot live
   under their own names.

   @{b}CAN'T KEEP UP in the player panel@{ub}

   The decoder is behind. Choose a lower quality, close programs that
   eat processor time (a module player with a scope, a web browser
   loading a page), hold off on downloads. See @{"Performance" link Performance}.

   @{b}"The episode is not MP3 and cannot be played on the Amiga"@{ub}

   The feed delivers AAC/M4A or video. It can be downloaded but not
   played on the Amiga.

@ENDNODE

@NODE KnownProblems "Known problems"
@TOC Main
@PREV Troubleshooting
@NEXT AppleDetails
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Known problems"

      - Episodes in AAC/M4A (audio/x-m4a, audio/mp4) can be downloaded
        but not played. There is no usable AAC decoder for 68k.

      - On the 030 config, searching the catalog while an MP3 is
        playing can end with "The TLS handshake failed": the decoder
        leaves too little time for the handshake. Pause, search, and
        play again.

      - A download halves the decoding speed on the 030, despite the
        lower priority. Download when nothing is playing, or live with
        it.

      - The position gauge is updated per buffer (0.7-1.4 seconds).

      - Skipping back and forward empties the buffers, so there is a
        short silence before the sound comes back.

      - Feeds with more than 300 episodes show only the latest 300;
        the list shows 50 by default.

      - An episode larger than 2 GB cannot be saved under FFS.

      - Searching with characters outside ISO-8859-1 goes wrong.

      - During 14-bit playback all four Paula channels are taken;
        HippoPlayer cannot play at the same time.

@ENDNODE

@NODE AppleDetails "Apple Podcasts details"
@TOC Main
@PREV KnownProblems
@NEXT Planned
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Apple Podcasts details"

   For those who want to know exactly what is sent and received.

   @{b}Catalog search@{ub}

   A search is a GET request:

      https://itunes.apple.com/search?media=podcast&entity=podcast
         &limit=50&country=se&term=retro+hour

   The reply is JSON. CurrentPod parses it with a small parser of its
   own and reads the fields collectionName, artistName, feedUrl,
   artworkUrl100, trackCount, releaseDate, primaryGenreName,
   collectionExplicitness and collectionId.

   @{b}Looking up an Apple link@{ub}

   The ID from the link is sent to

      https://itunes.apple.com/lookup?id=1073270208&entity=podcast

   which gives the same fields as above, in particular feedUrl. The
   Apple ID is saved in the .feed file (the Apple field).

   @{b}The itunes: namespace@{ub}

   Tags that are parsed, in order of priority against the basic RSS
   fields:

      Podcast: itunes:image (href) > image/url
               itunes:author > managingEditor
               itunes:summary / description (the longest wins)
               itunes:type (episodic/serial: serial shows oldest first)
      Episode: itunes:title > title
               itunes:duration (HH:MM:SS, MM:SS or seconds)
               itunes:episode, itunes:season
               itunes:episodeType (full/trailer/bonus)
               itunes:explicit
               itunes:image (episode artwork, saved as an address)
               content:encoded / description / itunes:summary
               (the longest wins)

   @{b}User-Agent@{ub}

   CurrentPod/1.0 (AmigaOS 3.1; 68k) - so that the server logs show
   there are Amigas out there.

@ENDNODE

@NODE Planned "Planned"
@TOC Main
@PREV AppleDetails
@NEXT Technical
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Planned"

   These were on the drawing board but did not make it into 1.0:

      - ARexx port (PLAY, PAUSE, NEXT, GETATTR POSITION...)
      - OPML import and export
      - Drag and drop of addresses onto the window
      - Automatic updates at an interval (the AutoUpdate field)
      - Automatic download of new episodes per podcast (the
        AutoDownload field exists in the database, but there is no
        setting)
      - Artwork in the lists (guigfx.library is on the machine)
      - Chapters (podcast:chapters and ID3v2 CHAP)
      - Top lists and genres in the search window
      - More search indexes: Podcast Index and fyyd.de
      - Streaming without downloading, sleep timer, playback speed
      - A settings window instead of the prefs file
      - A conversion queue (today one episode at a time) and "convert
        automatically after download" for machines without MP3 muscle
      - Delete the MP3 automatically when the 8SVX file is done

@ENDNODE

@NODE Technical "Technical"
@TOC Main
@PREV Planned
@NEXT License
@TITLE "CurrentPod - Technical"

   Written in C, cross-compiled on Linux with bebbo/AmigaPorts gcc 6.5
   (m68k-amigaos, libnix, -m68020 -m68881). The source code lives in
   src/currentpod on the host machine:

      main.c      startup, tooltypes, settings, stack switch
      gui.c       the MUI windows and the main loop
      worker.c    the network process: one job at a time (feed,
                  search, lookup, download)
      http.c      HTTP/HTTPS client over bsdsocket and AmiSSL
      rss.c       RSS/Atom parser with itunes: tags
      json.c      JSON reading for Apple's replies
      db.c        the database in PROGDIR:Feeds
      player.c    the player process: mpega.library -> audio.device,
                  or 8SVX from disk -> audio.device
      convert.c   the conversion process: mpega.library -> 8SVX file
      util.c      strings, UTF-8, dates, files

   Four processes: the main program (priority 0) owns the GUI and the
   database; the network process (-1) does all network I/O and parses
   feeds; the player process (+1) decodes and feeds Paula; the
   converter (-2) makes 8SVX files out of the time left over. They
   talk with Exec messages, and the player's status lives in a shared
   structure that the GUI reads once a second via timer.device.

   Lessons from the build, for anyone who wants to do something
   similar: the MUI SDK's inline MUI_NewObject takes the address of a
   varargs argument and crashes with gcc (CurrentPod has its own
   variant); subprocesses have no PROGDIR:; a library must be opened
   with the name that is stored inside the file; libnix reads %d as 16
   bits; and TLS 1.3 hangs in AmiSSL under FS-UAE.

@ENDNODE

